// std/verification.dag -- Verifiable claims about program behavior.
//
// A test is a named conjunction of verifiable claims. A claim is a
// proposition that a program state satisfies a predicate. This is the
// logical structure of verification — no testing philosophy, no
// fixtures, no mocking opinions.
//
// RELATIONSHIP TO std/termination.dag:
//   termination.dag proves well-founded descent via ranking functions.
//   This file proves specification compliance via test evidence.
//   Both are proof structures — the former for termination, the latter
//   for behavioral correctness.
//
// RELATIONSHIP TO std/logic.dag:
//   A TestClaim is a proposition (true or false). AssertKind encodes
//   the predicate form. A TestCase is a conjunction (all claims must hold).
//
// Consumers:
//   v2/coercion.dag         -- structural coercion tests from data declarations
//   v2/05_emit.dag          -- test extraction and rendering (future)

module std.verification

// The predicate form of an assertion.
// Each variant corresponds to a standard verification predicate
// that every test framework supports.
type AssertKind
  = AssertEqual    // actual == expected
  | AssertTrue     // condition holds
  | AssertSome     // value is present (not None/nil/null)
  | AssertNone     // value is absent

// A single verifiable claim about program behavior.
// The label describes what is being verified — it becomes the
// failure message when the claim does not hold.
type TestClaim {
  kind: AssertKind
  label: String
}

// A named test case — a conjunction of claims.
// All claims must hold for the test to pass.
type TestCase {
  name: String
  claims: List<TestClaim>
  ignored: Bool
}
